#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604016 :question/problem "![](images/d6/d691321f765094f492ec72076e96e13af0016f7b692cceba5f8d6aab21180f65.png)\n\nThe area of ​​the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{15\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$ Then,  $AC=${{field-1}} $\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=10\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC, \\\\ m∠A & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{15\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 10\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{15\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 10\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{15\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}\\,b}{2} \\\\ \\frac{15\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}}{\\cancel{2}} & =\\frac{5\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}\\,b}{\\cancel{2}} \\\\ 5b & =15 \\\\ b & =3\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=3\\,\\text{ cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604035 :question/problem "![](images/23/2345e527962c82c89c2689f17ee061ce2a6691ec58b21fff1890bed106ad5b5a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(5,\\pi )$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\pi ),$ we must have $r=5.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $5,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $5.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\pi ,$ the $\\theta$-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(5,\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604048 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\theta -\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-1,1).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{-1}\\right)| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)| \\\\  & =|-\\frac{\\pi }{4}| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the second quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(0,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604068 :question/problem "![](images/cc/cc376c3193693561bb65a6296fe8fb798f36f1b5f1152aba46c14a443887702c.png)\n\nThe area of $ABCD$ is $130\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.$   What is the area of $△BCD$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the area of $△ABD.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△ABD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot AD\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 24\\cdot 18\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{115}^{∘} \\\\  & =216\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{115}^{∘} \\\\  & =195.762… \\\\  & ≈196\\,{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of $△ABD$ and the quadrilateral $ABCD$ gives the area of $△BCD.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△BCD} & ={A}_{△ABD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =196-130 \\\\  & =66\\,{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604575 :question/problem "Find the dot product of the vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{w}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{w}=\\hspace{0.167em}$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The dot product of two vectors given in component form can be computed by summing up the products of corresponding components of vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {w}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {w}_{y}+{u}_{z}\\cdot {w}_{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom part 1, we have $u=⟨2,2,4⟩$ and $w=⟨0,0,2⟩.$ Therefore, the formula for the dot product gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =2\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0+0+8 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604612 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604621 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨3x-\\operatorname{cos}y,\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{7}+y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{5}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/00/00bceda1bdf4f515646b3a1e8476f1eadde721aa52dbc9be9c7e866bcab99d3b.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{7}+y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{5}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{5}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{{x}^{5}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & =4\\left(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}\\right){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186606800 :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(y,z)=⟨12{z}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{___}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(y,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $12{z}^{2}=x.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12{z}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $z.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186606802 :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,x)=⟨x,5\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,x)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${z}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse ${z}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1$ in $yz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨y,z⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+\\frac{(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{25} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,x) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186606807 :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\text{___}\\hspace{0.222em}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $5{y}^{2}=z.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,5{y}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $5{y}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186606810 :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,z)=⟨\\operatorname{tan}t,\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{_____},\\hspace{0.167em}z⟩,\\hspace{2em}t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,z)$ above parametrizes one sheet of the hyperbolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}-{x}^{2}=1$ for $y\\ge 6.$ What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}-{x}^{2}=1$ for $y\\ge 6$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}-{x}^{2} & =\\frac{(6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)^{2}}{36}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{36{sec}^{2}⁡t}{36}-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t-{tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186606814 :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,z)=⟨\\text{_____},\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.$ What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{16{cos}^{2}⁡t}{16}+\\frac{9{sin}^{2}⁡t}{9} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186606853 :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,z)=⟨\\text{_____},\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.$ What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+\\frac{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{9} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+\\frac{9{sin}^{2}⁡t}{9} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186606867 :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(y,z)=⟨10{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}y,\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{___}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(y,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x=10{y}^{2}.$ What is the third component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨10{y}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $z.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186606874 :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,z)=⟨x,\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{___},\\hspace{0.222em}z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(x,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $7{z}^{2}=y.$ What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,7{z}^{2},\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $7{z}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186606876 :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t,x)=⟨x,\\text{_____},\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}4\\operatorname{cos}t⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(t,x)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $16{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}=144.$ What is the second component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n16{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2}=144\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1\n$$\n\nin $yz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨y,z⟩=⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,x) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608215 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x{e}^{6x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{6x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{e}^{6x}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{6x}}{6}-\\int \\frac{{e}^{6x}}{6}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{x{e}^{6x}}{6}-\\frac{1}{6}\\int {e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{6x}}{6}-\\frac{{e}^{6x}}{36}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608222 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}5x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=5x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=5 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{3x} & ⇒ & v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 5x\\cdot {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =5x\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}5x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{3}x{e}^{3x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-\\frac{5}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}x{e}^{3x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-\\frac{5}{9}{e}^{3x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}({e}^{3}-0)-\\frac{5}{9}({e}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{3}-\\frac{5}{9}({e}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5(2{e}^{3}+1)}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186609024 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{t}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{11}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{t}+2\\mathbf{v}$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector and points the same direction, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{t+2v}{|t+2v|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t+2v & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{11}\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{11}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1+6 \\\\ \\sqrt{11}+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{11}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|t+v| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(\\sqrt{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $t+2v$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{t+2v}{|t+2v|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(t+2v) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186609837 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2,1,-4⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨4,0,k⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨-2,1,2⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0 & k \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & k \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & k \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot k)-(4\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot k)-4(4\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-2k-8-2k-16 \\\\  & =-4k-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ -4k-24 & =0 \\\\ 4k & =-24 \\\\ k & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186610506 :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(2 e^{x})\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=2 e^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=2 e^{x},\\quad v'(x)=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(2 e^{x})\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (2 e^{x})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=2 e^{x}(\\cos x+\\sin x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}} &=\\begin{aligned}&(2 e^{\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)})\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right) \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(\\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right) \\right)})\\cdot (2 e^{\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(2 e^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}})\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ &{}+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\cdot (2 e^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186610512 :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(e^{x})\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=e^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=e^{x},\\quad v'(x)=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(e^{x})\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (e^{x})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=e^{x}(\\cos x+\\sin x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=\\pi} &=\\begin{aligned}&(e^{(\\pi)})\\cdot (\\cos{\\left((\\pi) \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(\\sin{\\left((\\pi) \\right)})\\cdot (e^{(\\pi)})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=(e^{\\pi})\\cdot (-1)+(0)\\cdot (e^{\\pi}) \\\\ &=- e^{\\pi}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $- e^{\\pi}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186610517 :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(- x^{3})\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=- x^{3}$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=- 3 x^{2},\\quad v'(x)=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(- x^{3})\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (- 3 x^{2})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=- x^{2} \\left(x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} &=\\begin{aligned}&(- \\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right)^{3})\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(\\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)})\\cdot (- 3 \\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right)^{2})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&\\left(- \\frac{27 \\pi^{3}}{8}\\right)\\cdot (0) \\\\ &{}+(-1)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{27 \\pi^{2}}{4}\\right)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\frac{27 \\pi^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $\\frac{27 \\pi^{2}}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186610519 :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(4 x + 1)\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}),$ then the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=4 x + 1$ and $v(x)=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=4,\\quad v'(x)=- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(4 x + 1)\\cdot (- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (4)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=-(4 x + 1)\\sin x+4\\cos x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}} &=\\begin{aligned}&(4 \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + 1)\\cdot (- \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(\\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\right)})\\cdot (4)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(1 + \\pi)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ &{}+\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\cdot (4)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(3 - \\pi\\right)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(3 - \\pi\\right)}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186610528 :question/problem "Given $f(x)=- 3 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$, if a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=x\\cdot f(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\nSlope $\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=- 3 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=1,\\quad v'(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(x)\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(- 3 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\cdot (1)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\begin{aligned}&(x - 3)\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(- 3 x - 1)\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\end{aligned}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=\\frac{\\pi}{4}} &=\\begin{aligned}&(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right))\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\right)}) \\\\ &{}+(- 3 \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\right)})\\cdot (1)\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\cdot (- \\sqrt{2})+(- 2 \\sqrt{2})\\cdot (1) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(-8 - \\pi\\right)}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\left(-8 - \\pi\\right)}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186611693 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(1+2x)^{4},$ the equation of the tangent at the point $(-1,1)$ is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{4},\\,g=1+2x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4g^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=4g^{3}\\cdot (2) \\\\  &=8(1+2x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=-1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|_{x=-1} &=[8(1+2x)^{3}]|_{x=-1} \\\\  &=8(1+2(-1))^{3} \\\\  &=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(-1,1)$ is $m=-8.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(1) &=-8(x-(-1)) \\\\ y &=- 8 x - 8+1 \\\\ y &=- 8 x - 7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186611694 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(-4+2x)^{4},$ the equation of the tangent at the point $(1,16)$ is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{4},\\,g=-4+2x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4g^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=4g^{3}\\cdot (2) \\\\  &=8(-4+2x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|_{x=1} &=[8(-4+2x)^{3}]|_{x=1} \\\\  &=8(-4+2(1))^{3} \\\\  &=-64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,16)$ is $m=-64.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(16) &=-64(x-(1)) \\\\ y &=64 - 64 x+16 \\\\ y &=80 - 64 x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186611695 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(1+3x)^{4},$ the equation of the tangent at the point $(0,1)$ is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{4},\\,g=1+3x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4g^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=4g^{3}\\cdot (3) \\\\  &=12(1+3x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|_{x=0} &=[12(1+3x)^{3}]|_{x=0} \\\\  &=12(1+3(0))^{3} \\\\  &=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(0,1)$ is $m=12.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(1) &=12(x-(0)) \\\\ y &=12 x+1 \\\\ y &=12 x + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186613214 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(-1,-1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,-1)}x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1} & =(-1)\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186614572 :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}t=1+{\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}t & =1+{\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}t \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}t & =\\sqrt{1+{\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t>0$ and $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, substituting $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=x$ and $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we obtain\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{\\,1+{x}^{2}\\,}.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is \n$y=\\sqrt{\\,1+{x}^{2}\\,}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186614627 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=2t-3,\\,\\,\\,y=(t-4)(t+3),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x &=2t-3\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{x+3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{x+3}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(\\frac{x+3}{2}-4)(\\frac{x+3}{2}+3) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{x+3-8}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x+3+6}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{x-5}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{x+9}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+9)(x-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{1}{4}(x+9)(x-5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615386 :question/problem "The slope of the line is $m\\,=\\,${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Note that the line passes through the points $(0,960)$ and $(16,0).$ So, we let $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,960)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(16,0),$ and compute the slope using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-960}{16-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-960}{16} \\\\  & =-60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the line is $m=-60.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615399 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,0)$, can be found by computing $∇f(0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\text{ }i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,0) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\text{ }i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\text{ }j \\\\  & =j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(0,0)}{‖∇f(0,0)‖} & =\\frac{j}{1}=j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615429 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-x{y}^{3}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-x{y}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-x{y}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =(2xy-{y}^{3})\\,i+({x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(2(1)(1)-{1}^{3})\\,i+({1}^{2}-3(1)(1)^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,1)}{‖∇f(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{\\,i-2\\,j}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\,i-2\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615453 :question/problem "Mary wonders where and when they'd met if Sam left home $3.8$ minutes earlier and walked with a speed of $50$ meters per minute. She finds that the distance $d$ from home they'd meet can be modeled using the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}d+60t &=960 \\\\ d-50t &=190.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time (in minutes) since Mary left the community park.\n\nMary and Sam would meet$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$minutes after Mary left the community park.\n\nMary and Sam would meet at a distance of $\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,$meters from home." :question/worked-solution "To solve the given system of linear equations, we will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the second equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $d$ adding to both sides of the equation $50t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d-50t &=190 \\\\ d &=190+50t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $d$ with $190+50t$ in the first equation and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d+60t=960 \\\\ (190+50t)+60t & =960 \\\\ 190+110t & =960 \\\\ 110t & =960-190 \\\\ 110t & =770 \\\\ t & =\\frac{770}{110} \\\\ t & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, they would have met after $t=7$ minutes.\n\nIf we substitute $t=7$ into $d=190+50t$, we can find the value of $d:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =190+50t \\\\  & =190+50(7) \\\\  & =540\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, they meet at a distance of $540$ meters from home."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615560 :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(2 e^{x})\\cdot (x - 1)$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=2 e^{x}$ and $v(x)=x - 1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\nu'(x)=2 e^{x},\\quad v'(x)=1.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx} &=uv'(x)+vu'(x) \\\\ &=(2 e^{x})\\cdot (1)+(x - 1)\\cdot (2 e^{x}) \\\\ &=2 x e^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left.\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}\\right|_{x=0} &=\\begin{aligned}&(2 e^{(0)})\\cdot (1) \\\\ &{}+((0) - 1)\\cdot (2 e^{(0)})\\end{aligned} \\\\ &=(2)\\cdot (1)+(-1)\\cdot (2) \\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616134 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=5-4x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=5-4x.$\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=5-4(0)=5,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=5-4(1)=1,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=5-4(2)=-3,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=5-4(3)=-7.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $1$ | $-3$ | $-7$ |\n\nPlotting these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane: \n\n![](images/d8/d8b81418ee13fbf6ceda3bdb40b418db13fc751082bdcf861777a2935fd39209.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=5-4x.$\n\n![](images/d4/d42d80f53ee904ea12475ae64e9bde55845b7c9974a303535882f6f385478abd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616135 :question/problem "![](images/8c/8cfcac21fcb0fd0f40d5992d3632478b5eb9b459bc26c3769bc10ad0dedd7ddf.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=x+2.$" :question/worked-solution "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(2,4),B(4,2)$ and $C(5,7).$ \n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(2,4)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ (4) & =(2)+2 \\\\ 4 & =4\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(4,2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ (2) & =(4)+2 \\\\ 2 & =6\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(5,7)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\ (7) & =(5)+2 \\\\ 7 & =7\\hspace{0.278em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616136 :question/problem "![](images/9e/9ed1cae3079318854aa49cd1fc41fc750d0abfb76f2c125d3801bae19b0d2bce.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx=3t{e}^{t},\\,y={t}^{2}+4t,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nfind the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,2}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{t}(t+1),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t+4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2t+4}{3{e}^{t}(t+1)}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}{|}_{t\\,=\\,2} & =\\frac{2(2)+4}{3{e}^{2}(2+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4}{3{e}^{2}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616153 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=x-3.$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=x-3.$\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=(2)-3=-1$;\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=(3)-3=0$;\n\n- if $x=4$ then $y=(4)-3=1$;\n\n- if $x=5$ then $y=(5)-3=2.$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nPlotting these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane would look like this: \n\n![](images/1f/1fe5f6ab820a248c9b2dbcee4e09a9ad80fa46baaf80d4d2ebe7f9978f67825e.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line $y=x-3.$\n\n![](images/90/909b704166ab6bcd78ed60fd758c09b203060a36c63a27b91f70a70884413403.png)"}]}
